    "### TEST 2026-10-07 (B): pure tones at each L_6 filter's frequency\n",
    "\n",
    "**What it does.** For each `L_6` filter $j$ it builds a pure tone at the filter's peak frequency, a whole number of periods on the $M$ points (periods 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256 samples; the low-pass filter gets a constant signal). The amplitude grows from 0.1 to 100 × the data std. For each seed it computes $\\log p = \\theta\\cdot\\varphi(x)$ using **all** families, not only `L_6`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to read it.** For $p_\\theta$ to be normalisable along that oscillation, $\\log p$ must go **down** without limit as the amplitude grows. A curve that goes **up** at large amplitude means $p_\\theta$ puts unbounded mass there.\n",
    "- `growth power` ≈ the power of the term that wins at large amplitude: 6 = `L_6`, 4 = Scat4, $\\alpha$ = a Scalar outermost region.\n",
    "- `largest family at a_max` says which family that is.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Which explanation fits.**\n",
    "- **Coarse L_6 poorly determined** (current best guess): which filters rise changes from seed to seed.\n",
    "- **Coarse scales not held back by anything else** (only `L_2_lowpass` is quadratic there): the same filters rise in all seeds.\n",
    "- **Another family wins at large amplitude**: the coarse L_6 sign doesn't matter."
   ]
  },
